{
 "cells": [
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "# Homework 4\r\n\r\n## Introduction\r\n\r\nBased on Landau's previously established theory of the second-order phase transitions, Ginzburg and Landau argued that the free energy, F, of a superconductor near the superconducting transition can be expressed in terms of a complex order paramtere field, ψ(r) = |ψ(r)|eiϕ(r), where the quantity |ψ(r)|^2 is a measure of the local density, like a quantum mechanics wave function and ψ(r) is a nonzero below a phase transition into a superconducting state, although no direct interpretation of this parameter was given in the original paper. Assuming smallness of the |ψ| and smallness of its gradient s, the free energy has the form of a field theory.\r\n\r\nF = Fn + α|ψ|^2 + (β/2)|ψ|^4 + 1/(2m*)|(−iℏ∇ - q*/c A) ψ|^2 + |B|^2/8π\r\n\r\nwhere Fn is the free energy in the normal phase, α and β are treated as phenominological parameters, m is an effective mass**, A is the magnetic vector potential, and B = ∇ x A is the magnetic field.\r\n\r\n- **Note: m = 2me, where me is the mass of an electron\r\n\r\nBy minimizing the free energy with respect to variations in the order paramter and vector potential, one arrives at the Ginzburg-Landau equations\r\n\r\nαψ + β|ψ|^2 ψ + 1/(2m) (−iℏ∇ - q*/c A)^2 ψ = 0\r\n\r\n∇ x B = 4π/j ; j = -(iq'ℏ)/(2m*) {ψ∗ ∇ψ − ψ ∇ψ∗} - ((q')^2|ψ|^2)/(m*c) A\r\n\r\nwhere J denotes the dissipationless electric current density and Re. The first equation determines the order paramter, ψ. The second provides the superconducting current.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {},
   "executionSummary": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## Exercise 1\r\n\r\nUse Mathematica to obtain the Ginzburg Landau equations from the minimization of the free energy.\r\n\r\n### Note\r\n\r\nThe analysis leading to the GL equation can be done in terms of either the Helmholtz or Gibbs free energy.\r\n\r\n- The Helmholtz free energy is considered for situations when B = < H >;<br>macroscopic average is held constant rather than H, because if B is constant, there is no induced emf and no energy input from the current generator.\r\n\r\n- The Gibbs free energy is considered for situations when H is constant.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {},
   "executionSummary": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "<< VariationalMethods`",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {},
   "executionSummary": {}
  },
