      "isBoxData": false
     }
    }
   ],
   "metadata": {},
   "executionSummary": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## Homogenous superconductor\r\n\r\nConsider a homogenous superconductor where there is no superconducting currrent and the equation for ψ simplfies to:\r\n\r\nαψ + β|ψ|^2 ψ = 0\r\n\r\nThis equation has a trivial solution: ψ = 0. This corresponds to the normal conducting state, that is for temperatures above the superconducting transition temperature, T > Tc.\r\n\r\nBelow the superconducting transition temperature, the above equation is expected to have a non-trivial solution (of ψ =/= 0). Under this assumption the equation can ve rearranged to:\r\n\r\n|ψ|^2 = − α / β\r\n\r\nWhen the right hand side of the equaation is positive, there is nonzero solution for ψ. This can be achieved assumong the following temperature dependence of α:\r\n\r\nα(T) = α0(T - Tc), for α / β > 0\r\n\r\nAbove the superconducting transition temprature, T > Tc, α(T)/β is positive and the right hand side of the equation above is negative. The magnitude of a complex number must be a non-negative number, so only ψ = 0 solves the Ginzburg-Landau equation.\r\n\r\nBelow the superconducting temperature, T < Tc, the right hand side of the equation above is positive and there is a non-trivial sulotion for ψ. Furthermore:\r\n\r\n|ψ|^2 = - α0(T - Tc) / β\r\n\r\nthat is ψ approaches zero as T gets closer to Tc from below. Such behaviour is typical for a second order phase transition.\r\n\r\nIn Ginzburg-Landau theory the electrons that contribute to superconductivity were proposed to form a superfluid. In this interpretation |ψ|^2 indicates the fraction of electrons that have condensed into a superfluid.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {},
   "executionSummary": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## Exercise 2\r\n\r\n- Plot the Free energy for several temperatures\r\n- Plot extreme solutions as functions of the temperature",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {},
   "executionSummary": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "(* Plot the Free energy for several temperatures *)\r\n\r\n(* Define parameters *)\r\nalpha0 = 2; (* Coefficient for alpha(T) *)\r\nbeta = 1;   (* Coefficient for the quartic term *)\r\nTc = 1;   (* Critical temperature *)\r\n\r\n(* Define alpha(T) *)\r\nalpha[T_] := alpha0 (T - Tc)\r\n\r\n(* Define the free energy density F *)\r\nF[T_, psi_] := alpha[T] Abs[psi]^2 + (1/2) beta Abs[psi]^4\r\n\r\n(* Plot the free energy for several temperatures *)\r\ntemperatures = {Tc + 2, Tc, Tc - 2}; (* T > Tc, T = Tc, T < Tc *)\r\nPlot[\r\n  Evaluate@Table[F[T, ψ], {T, 0.5, 1.52, 0.25}], \r\n  {ψ, -2.5, 2.5}, \r\n  PlotLegends -> Table[\"T = \" <> ToString[T], {T, 0.5, 1.5, 0.25}],\r\n  AxesLabel -> {\"ψ\", \"F\"},\r\n  PlotLabel -> \"Free Energy for several Temperatures\"\r\n]",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [
    {
     "items": [
      {
